%S A401908 9,2,4,1,2,3,2,1,6,2,2,2,1,2,1,2,4,1,4,2,1,1,1,2,3,1,4,1,1,3,1,1,1,2,
%T A401908 1,2,1,2,2,2,1,4,1,2,2,1,2,1,1,1,1,2,1,1,1,2,1,3,1,3,1,2,2,1,1,1,1,1,
%U A401908 1,1,2,1,2,2,1,1,2,4,1,4,4,2,1,1,2,1,1
%N A401908 Number of primes in the n-th maximal cluster of primes with consecutive gaps at most 4.
%C A401908 The initial count a(1)=9 is the unique maximum since every later cluster contains at most 6 primes (A399821).
%C A401908 For jump limit 8, whether a later cluster equals or exceeds the initial count 30 remains unresolved (A399819).
%C A401908 For jump limit 12, the initial count 30 is exceeded by the cluster 127 to 293, containing 32 primes.
%H A401908 Jwalin Bhatt, <a href="/A401908/b401908.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A401908 The first cluster is {2,3,5,7,11,13,17,19,23} and contains 9 primes, so a(1)=9. The next two clusters are {29,31} and {37,41,43,47}, giving a(2)=2 and a(3)=4. The fourth cluster is {53}, so a(4)=1.
%t A401908 Map[Length, Most[Split[Prime[Range[200]], #2 - # <= 4 &]]] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Nov 15 2026 *)
%o A401908 (Python)
%o A401908 from sympy import nextprime
%o A401908 from itertools import islice
%o A401908 def gen_A401908():
%o A401908     p, count = 2, 1
%o A401908     while True:
%o A401908         q = int(nextprime(p))
%o A401908         if q - p > 4:
%o A401908             yield count
%o A401908             count = 0
%o A401908         count += 1
%o A401908         p = q
%o A401908 print(list(islice(gen_A401908(), 87)))
%Y A401908 Cf. A000040, A000720, A001223, A399819, A399821, A401842.
%K A401908 nonn,new
%O A401908 1,1
